WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.418
Book Three, Proposition Twenty.

2
00:00:02.418 --> 00:00:11.054
In a circle, the angle at the center is double of the angle at the circumference, when the angles have the same circumference as base.

3
00:00:11.054 --> 00:00:21.043
Let A B C be a circle, let the angle B E C be an angle at its center E, and the angle B A C an angle at the circumference, having the same arc B C as base.

4
00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:24.330
We claim that angle B E C is double of angle B A C.

5
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.319
Join A E and draw it through to F.

6
00:00:27.319 --> 00:00:36.160
And for the second case, let another angle B D C be inflected at the circumference, and join D E and produce it to G.

7
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:50.690
Since the radius E A equals the radius E B, by One Five the base angle E A B equals the base angle E B A; therefore the sum of angles E A B and E B A is double of angle E A B.

8
00:00:50.690 --> 00:01:05.284
But by One Thirty-Two, the exterior angle B E F of triangle E A B equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles E A B and E B A; therefore angle B E F is double of angle E A B.

9
00:01:05.284 --> 00:01:12.540
For the same reason, in triangle E A C, the exterior angle F E C is double of angle E A C.

10
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:19.394
Therefore the whole central angle B E C is double of the whole inscribed angle B A C.

11
00:01:19.394 --> 00:01:36.164
Again, for the inflected angle B D C, angle G E C is similarly double of angle E D C, of which angle G E B is double of angle E D B; therefore the remaining angle B E C is double of the remaining angle B D C.

12
00:01:36.164 --> 00:01:38.180
As was required to prove.

13
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:45.841
Proposition Three Twenty is the celebrated Inscribed Angle Theorem, one of the most fertile hubs in all of Euclid's Elements.

14
00:01:45.841 --> 00:01:59.627
Because classical Greek geometry restricted plane angles to inclinations less than two right angles, Heron of Alexandria later supplied the extension to segments smaller than a semicircle by summing the remaining angles around the center.

15
00:01:59.627 --> 00:02:16.600
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred twenty-three unites the isosceles triangle theorem One Five and exterior angle theorem One Thirty-Two with circular sectors, directly powering one hundred thirty-seven downstream propositions across Books Three, Four, Six, Ten, Twelve, and Thirteen.

16
